.TH TRAC 1
.SH NAME
trac \- Mooers' TRAC macro-language interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trac
.I file.tc
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Trac
is a work-alike of Mooers' TRAC (Text Reckoning And Compiler, 1964),
one of the languages named in Nelson's
.IR "Computer Lib/Dream Machines" :
a pure string-rewriting language. A
.I form
.B #(name,arg,arg,...)
is replaced in place by whatever it evaluates to, and the replacement
text is itself rescanned \(em there is no loop construct at all;
recursion via a function calling itself, guarded by a comparison, is
how a TRAC program loops.
.PP
Forms come in two kinds, distinguished by how many
.B #
precede the
.BR ( :
.B #(...)
is
.I active
\(em evaluated immediately as the scanner reaches it.
.B ##(...)
is
.I neutral
\(em the scanner strips one
.B #
and copies everything up to the matching
.B )
through untouched, so it sits inert until something later rescans
that text. This is what lets
.B ds
store a function body containing a self-call without it firing at
definition time.
.PP
Primitives:
.BR ds ,
.BR ss ,
.B cl
(define, mark parameter positions by literal substring, invoke \(em
though calling any name directly does the same lookup),
.B dd
(delete),
.B eq / gr
(string-equal / numeric-greater-than conditionals),
.B ad / su / ml / dv
(integer arithmetic),
.B ln / cc / cn
(length, first character, first
.I n
characters), and
.B ps / rs
(print \(em the only primitives with an immediate side effect \(em and
read a line from the terminal).
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/trac/fizzbuzz.tc
.TP
.B /lib/trac/hello.tc
Minimal ds/ss/cl template-substitution demo.
.TP
.B /lib/trac/fizzbuzz.tc
Recursion, arithmetic, and branching all doing real work: a
self-recursive loop counting 1 to 15, printing fizz/buzz/fizzbuzz.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/trac.b
